1
00:00:00,441 --> 00:00:03,470
A robot stands beside a person at a workbench.

2
00:00:03,580 --> 00:00:09,808
It could perform the entire task itself, or clear an obstruction so the person can choose what to build.

3
00:00:09,919 --> 00:00:15,707
Both might change the environment, but only one necessarily increases the person's control.

4
00:00:15,817 --> 00:00:21,171
Before writing an empowerment objective, identify whose action is the input to the channel.

5
00:00:21,446 --> 00:00:27,491
Learning to Assist Humans without Inferring Rewards places the human's current action on that input side.

6
00:00:27,601 --> 00:00:33,882
The assistant changes the environment and therefore changes how human actions influence future states.

7
00:00:33,993 --> 00:00:41,605
The objective is to learn an assistant that improves this influence over time, rather than explicitly reconstructing a hidden human

8
00:00:41,715 --> 00:00:42,823
reward function.

9
00:00:43,099 --> 00:00:46,232
This does not mean the method knows nothing about humans.

10
00:00:46,342 --> 00:00:49,615
It learns from their behavior and the resulting transitions.

11
00:00:49,725 --> 00:00:56,392
The distinction is between estimating a reward function and estimating the control consequences of human actions.

12
00:00:56,502 --> 00:01:04,624
Our toy workbench lets us vary those consequences directly, so we can inspect the objective before considering a learned estimator.

13
00:01:05,891 --> 00:01:11,965
Return to two reliable buttons, but now suppose a person almost always presses the left one.

14
00:01:12,075 --> 00:01:15,134
The machine still supports two distinct outcomes.

15
00:01:15,244 --> 00:01:17,723
Its channel capacity remains one bit.

16
00:01:17,833 --> 00:01:25,784
Yet the information realized under this person's biased action distribution is smaller, because there is less uncertainty about which

17
00:01:25,894 --> 00:01:27,089
button they will press.

18
00:01:27,365 --> 00:01:32,648
That is the difference between potential capacity and effective empowerment under a policy.

19
00:01:32,758 --> 00:01:41,942
At the extreme, if the person always chooses the same action, its entropy is zero, so its mutual information with any future is also

20
00:01:42,052 --> 00:01:42,572
zero.

21
00:01:42,682 --> 00:01:51,243
The selected action could still accomplish an important task; this metric measures a particular form of influence, not usefulness in

22
00:01:51,354 --> 00:01:52,271
every sense.

23
00:01:52,547 --> 00:01:54,953
The lab makes that dependence explicit.

24
00:01:55,063 --> 00:01:59,546
Vary the human's action preference while holding the transition channel fixed.

25
00:01:59,657 --> 00:02:02,982
Then remove an obstruction or increase assistant takeover.

26
00:02:03,092 --> 00:02:09,106
Capacity, realized information, and success on one chosen outcome need not move together.

27
00:02:09,216 --> 00:02:13,859
That disagreement is a diagnostic feature of the model, not a plotting error.

28
00:02:15,127 --> 00:02:21,384
To estimate the objective in a complex environment, the paper learns two predictions of the same future.

29
00:02:21,494 --> 00:02:26,485
The controlled prediction sees the state, assistant action, and human action.

30
00:02:26,595 --> 00:02:32,239
The comparison prediction sees the state and assistant action but averages over the human action.

31
00:02:32,349 --> 00:02:35,060
Their difference asks what the human's action adds.

32
00:02:35,336 --> 00:02:41,234
Contrastive learning can estimate density ratios without explicitly modeling every future state.

33
00:02:41,345 --> 00:02:48,548
Positive pairs connect a context with a future reached from it; negative pairs provide a reference future distribution.

34
00:02:48,659 --> 00:02:52,161
A representation inner product scores their compatibility.

35
00:02:52,271 --> 00:02:58,849
The paper trains two symmetrized contrastive objectives, sharing a representation of the future state.

36
00:02:59,125 --> 00:03:06,362
At the ideal optimum, the scores correspond to log density ratios up to the constants described in the analysis.

37
00:03:06,472 --> 00:03:12,791
Those ideal conditions matter: arbitrary uncalibrated logits need not represent probabilities.

38
00:03:12,902 --> 00:03:21,280
In the exact lab calculation, we construct both ratios from known tables, giving us a reference against which to understand the learned

39
00:03:21,390 --> 00:03:22,461
approximation.

40
00:03:23,729 --> 00:03:26,521
Subtract the two ideal log-ratio scores.

41
00:03:26,631 --> 00:03:29,985
The reference future density appears in both and cancels.

42
00:03:30,095 --> 00:03:37,941
What remains is the log probability of the future with the human action known, divided by its probability when the human action is not

43
00:03:38,052 --> 00:03:38,520
known.

44
00:03:38,630 --> 00:03:43,734
This is exactly the pointwise evidence term inside conditional mutual information.

45
00:03:44,010 --> 00:03:49,726
Average that term under the appropriate joint distribution of human actions and resulting futures.

46
00:03:49,836 --> 00:03:55,017
The expectation becomes effective empowerment, up to the ideal-model constants.

47
00:03:55,127 --> 00:04:03,283
Notice the distinction between a sampled reward and its expectation: a single transition can have negative evidence even though mutual

48
00:04:03,393 --> 00:04:05,552
information itself is nonnegative.

49
00:04:05,828 --> 00:04:14,935
Empowerment via Successor Representations, or ESR, uses the representation-score difference as an intrinsic reward for training the

50
00:04:15,045 --> 00:04:15,758
assistant.

51
00:04:15,868 --> 00:04:21,149
It alternates data collection, representation learning, and reinforcement learning.

52
00:04:21,260 --> 00:04:30,585
In a real implementation, changing either policy changes the data distribution, so stale representations can produce misleading rewards.

53
00:04:30,695 --> 00:04:36,464
The exact channel model lets us isolate the cancellation before introducing those learning issues.

54
00:04:37,732 --> 00:04:41,302
Consider three desired destinations behind an obstruction.

55
00:04:41,412 --> 00:04:45,548
Without help, several human actions lead to the same blocked state.

56
00:04:45,658 --> 00:04:52,512
Removing the obstruction can separate their outcome distributions, making the human's choices more consequential.

57
00:04:52,622 --> 00:04:59,543
The assistant has increased influence by changing the channel, without needing to know which destination will be preferred.

58
00:04:59,818 --> 00:05:07,347
Now imagine an assistant that always delivers the object to one predetermined destination, regardless of the person's action.

59
00:05:07,458 --> 00:05:12,368
It might score perfectly when that destination happens to be the evaluation target.

60
00:05:12,478 --> 00:05:16,577
But the output no longer carries information about the person's choice.

61
00:05:16,688 --> 00:05:20,728
In our simplified model, effective empowerment falls to zero.

62
00:05:21,003 --> 00:05:23,279
This example is deliberately narrow.

63
00:05:23,389 --> 00:05:30,768
Real assistance may include constraints, shared plans, safety requirements, and legitimate delegation.

64
00:05:30,878 --> 00:05:34,470
An information objective alone does not settle those questions.

65
00:05:34,580 --> 00:05:40,955
What it provides is a measurable distinction between changing the world and making another agent's choices effective.

66
00:05:41,066 --> 00:05:46,361
That distinction is often invisible if we report only one fixed task's completion rate.

67
00:05:47,629 --> 00:05:56,420
The paper connects empowerment to an average-case reward quantity under explicit assumptions: a Boltzmann-rational human, a specified

68
00:05:56,530 --> 00:06:01,682
reward-coverage distribution, ergodicity, and a sufficiently long horizon.

69
00:06:01,793 --> 00:06:04,875
This is a conditional justification for the proxy.

70
00:06:04,985 --> 00:06:10,906
It is not a theorem that maximizing the estimate will satisfy every individual human preference.

71
00:06:11,182 --> 00:06:15,146
The experiments include synthetic settings and a cooperative game.

72
00:06:15,256 --> 00:06:21,720
For a new application, inspect human behavior as well as estimator values and task outcomes.

73
00:06:21,830 --> 00:06:29,248
A changing human policy, missing options in the data, or a poor representation can alter what the learned score means.

74
00:06:29,359 --> 00:06:35,552
Compare with strong assistance baselines rather than assuming that more mutual information is always better.

75
00:06:35,827 --> 00:06:44,529
The conceptual step is nevertheless powerful: a robot can learn to improve the consequences of human choices without first identifying

76
00:06:44,639 --> 00:06:46,491
an explicit reward function.

77
00:06:46,601 --> 00:06:50,692
In the final lesson, the environment becomes a text document.

78
00:06:50,803 --> 00:06:58,391
The human's action is an edit, and the assistant must learn which predictable work to complete before returning control at a meaningful

79
00:06:58,501 --> 00:06:59,173
decision.
